Consider $\varphi$ as the golden ratio, defined as $\varphi=\frac{1+\sqrt{5}}{2}.$&lt;br&gt;
Intriguingly, it is feasible to express every positive integer as a summation of powers of $\varphi$, even when we stipulate that each power of $\varphi$ is utilized at most once in this summation.&lt;br&gt;
Even under these conditions, this representation lacks uniqueness.&lt;br&gt;
We can achieve uniqueness by imposing that no powers with successive exponents are employed and that the representation is finite.&lt;br&gt;
For instance: 
$2=\varphi+\varphi^{-2}$ and $3=\varphi^{2}+\varphi^{-2}$
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
To depict this summation of powers of $\varphi$, we employ a string of 0&#39;s and 1&#39;s with a point to signify where the negative exponents commence.&lt;br&gt;
We refer to this as the representation in the &lt;b&gt;phigital numberbase&lt;/b&gt;.&lt;br&gt;
Hence, $1=1_{\varphi}$, $2=10.01_{\varphi}$, $3=100.01_{\varphi}$ and $14=100100.001001_{\varphi}$. &lt;br&gt;
The strings representing $1$, $2$ and $14$ in the phigital number base are palindromic, whereas the string representing $3$ is not.&lt;br&gt; (the phigital point is not the central character).
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
The summation of the positive integers not surpassing $1000$ whose phigital representation is palindromic amounts to $4345$.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
Determine the summation of the positive integers not exceeding $10^{10}$ whose phigital representation is palindromic.&lt;/p&gt;

